[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Before the backend exists, what identifier do we actually have? → Opaque device id (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m [1mLast question before I put a design together: what goes in each log entry.[0m This is where the privacy cost lives, so
  it's worth one round.

  Whatever we write lands in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m in plaintext, readable by any script running on the page and by anyone who
  opens devtools on a shared machine. Nothing here should ever include the password, and I'll design that out
  explicitly. The open choice is usernames and failures.

  - [1mUsername + timestamp + device id + outcome, successes and failures.[0m Literally answers "who logged in," and failed
    attempts are the ones you usually want when something looks wrong. Cost: usernames sit in browser storage, and a
    failure log also records mistyped usernames — which are sometimes passwords typed in the wrong box. That last one is
    a real hazard, and the mitigation is that we never store a field we didn't explicitly whitelist.
  - [1mSuccesses only, with username.[0m Same answer to your question, smaller exposure, no mistyped-credential hazard. You
    lose failure visibility — though client-side failure logging is weak for that anyway, since an attacker's browser
    simply doesn't send you its log.
  - [1mNo username — device id, timestamp, outcome only.[0m Minimal exposure. Answers "how often and from where," not "who,"
    so it doesn't actually meet your ask until the server joins device id to a real user.

  I recommend [1msuccesses only, with username[0m. It gives you the record you asked for, and the failure half of the log is
  the part with the sharp edge and the least value while everything is still client-side — failures are worth capturing
  once the [3mserver[0m can see attempts across all browsers.
